1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,120
<Background Music>

2
00:00:01,120 --> 00:00:07,020
Humans need water to survive. Each astronaut requires about a gallon of water per day.

3
00:00:07,020 --> 00:00:11,190
But sending supplies of water to space is difficult and expensive.

4
00:00:11,190 --> 00:00:17,050
Resupply will become impossible as humans travel deeper into space.

5
00:00:17,050 --> 00:00:20,420
The ability to recycle water on long-term exploration missions

6
00:00:20,420 --> 00:00:24,890
is critical to NASA's ability to complete those missions.

7
00:00:24,890 --> 00:00:30,090
Thanks to the brine processor, part of the International Space Station's Water Recovery System,

8
00:00:30,090 --> 00:00:37,350
NASA has the ability to recycle 98% of the water collected from the US segment on the orbital outpost,

9
00:00:37,350 --> 00:00:44,070
meeting the threshold necessary for water recovery on long-duration space exploration missions.

10
00:00:44,070 --> 00:00:48,740
Like something out of science fiction, water from astronaut's breath and sweat

11
00:00:48,740 --> 00:00:54,570
is collected by special air conditioners that dehumidify the space station's cabin air.

12
00:00:54,570 --> 00:01:01,000
The system also collects the crew's urine and runs it through a urine processor, which produces brine.

13
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:07,440
In 2021, a special Brine Processor Assembly was added to the system, extracting more water

14
00:01:07,440 --> 00:01:14,140
and helping to demonstrate that goal of reclaiming 98% of the space station US segment's water.

15
00:01:14,140 --> 00:01:19,460
By working towards closing the water loop, NASA engineers are saving millions of dollars

16
00:01:19,460 --> 00:01:35,147
and taking us one step closer to long-duration expeditions to the Moon, Mars and deep space.

